From 2f07603077db94f2e56f8e2344ffae496c098c1a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Jul 2026 09:53:50 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07632/11362] [outer-loop] fix
 GAP-ARC-BP35-CLICK-CANDIDATE-GENERATION-MISS (opt-in, partial)

Operator: "yes" to fixing bp35's characterized click-candidate
generation gap from the candidate-coverage attribution replication.

Root cause (found via direct tracing): object_centric_digest (the
function rich_action_candidates actually uses by default -- an
initial assumption that arc_color_blob_salience.connected_color_blobs
was the live path was wrong, corrected mid-session) excludes the
single most-common color wholesale as "background," unconditionally.
bp35's color 5 (2109/4096px, one connected component) is that color,
so none of its pixels ever segment into a clickable object.

Fix: object_centric_digest gained emit_grid_fallback_for_background
(opt-in, CARNOT_ARC_GRID_FALLBACK_CANDIDATES=1, default off) -- tiles
the excluded background mask on an 8px grid into small
is_grid_fallback candidates, fails closed above a 64-tile cap.
rich_action_candidates gives these a dedicated reserved budget,
separate from the normal salience sort.

Two real bugs found and fixed while measuring: (1) an initial version
pooled fallback tiles into the same salience sort as real objects --
since fallback tiles are by construction low-rarity, they always
sorted to the bottom and got cut by max_click=48, measured 55
generated / 0 surviving. Fixed by reserving a dedicated budget.
(2) sorting that budget largest-first also failed -- the tile
covering a real winning click was small (area=9) and sat behind ~50
large empty tiles; flipped to smallest-first (mirrors
REQ-ARC-FCP-5758's own established finding) and it worked.

Measured on bp35's real reproduction-gated winning trajectory: 21/57
generation misses -> 15/57 at the real default max_click=48 -- a
genuine, disclosed PARTIAL fix (~29%), not complete. An uncapped
max_click=200 diagnostic closed it completely (0/57), confirming the
tiling mechanism itself is sound; the residual misses are a real
candidate-budget-sharing tradeoff, not a defect. lf52/re86 (0 misses
either way) measured byte-for-byte unaffected on and off.

A real backward-compat break was found and fixed: several existing
tests monkeypatch _components_detailed with the pre-fix 4-tuple
return shape. New code unpacks defensively (len(c) > 4) rather than
assuming 5-tuples. Caught by running the FULL surface-area regression
suite, not just new tests -- 14 failures on first pass, all fixed;
confirmed the remaining 8 are pre-existing via a clean-HEAD baseline.

Deliberately still opt-in, not the new default -- a partial fix on
one game's empirical tile pitch, unvalidated broadly. Wiring into the
live default is a separate follow-up decision.

